## 1. The pinned formula, and the literature's name for it

Our surviving model class:

```
c_m[t] = C[(σ·p_m[t] + base_m + drift·t) mod N]        N = 83, σ ∈ {±1}, drift ∈ {+1,0,−1}
```

The 2020 Kaeding analysis of "cipher clocks" (Wheatstone Cryptograph, Wadsworth
disk, Urkryptografen) factors that entire device class as

```
E(P) = M(S(M′(P)))
```

— a **keyless stream cipher `S` followed by a monoalphabetic substitution `M`**,
with an optional inner substitution `M′` when the plaintext ring is also mixed.
That is *exactly* our shape: `M = C` (the mixed alphabet, the key), `M′ = E` (the
plaintext letter→code embedding), and `S` = the index-space stream
`σp + base + drift·t`. Two of Kaeding's structural observations transfer verbatim:

- **Rotation gauge.** "Starting the cipher from an initial state that is not zero
  is equivalent to rotating the mixed ciphertext alphabet… if we cut a ciphertext
  at any point, then from that point onward it is as if it had been enciphered
  with a rotated key." This is our `base_m` gauge — per-message bases are key
  rotations, which is why every crib/pin result in this repo is "up to one
  rotation."
- **The hard case is ours.** The automated ciphertext-only attack achieves >90%
  success at ~125 characters for Wheatstone/Urkryptografen and ~250 for Wadsworth
  — but only with a **known plaintext ring**. Friedman (1918) proposed the
  double-mixed variant (both rings mixed); Kaeding states plainly that his attack
  **cannot break the double-mixed device with random alphabets**, and Friedman's
  own pen-and-paper attack on it relied on keyword-derived (non-random) alphabets.
  Our unknown-`E` × unknown-`C` situation *is* the double-mixed clock. The wall
  we hit (order_anneal floor ≈ shuffled floor) is the same wall the literature
  documents for this exact configuration. Our extra assets — 9 messages, triplet
  depth, isomorph anchors — are things the clock literature never had.

Within the hand-cipher (ACA) nomenclature the same object is a **Quagmire-family
substitution driven by a progressive (Trithemius-style) key instead of a periodic
indicator keyword**; NCID's 55-type battery contains our whole neighborhood as
named types: `progressive_key`, `quagmire2/3/4`, `gromark`, `periodic_gromark`,
`beaufort`, `porta`, `vigenere`.

## 2. Taxonomy — the families, their recurrences, and their tells

| Family | Recurrence (letters as ℤ_N codes) | Primary ciphertext tells | Eye-corpus verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Monoalphabetic | `c = C[p]` | Rough unigram histogram (language IoC survives) | **Excluded** — unigrams flat (repo) |
| Periodic Vigenère / Quagmire I–IV | `c = C_k[p]`, `k = t mod L` | **Kasiski repeats at multiples of L; periodic IoC spike at L**; per-column language IoC | **Excluded** — no Kasiski / periodic IoC signal at any period (repo sweeps) |
| Progressive key (Trithemius; ACA `progressive_key`) | `c = C[(p + t) mod N]` (+ key) | No fixed period; **repeats appear as isomorphs, not literal repeats**; coincidence spectrum diagonal effects (§4) | **Leading class** (with per-message base) |
| Cipher clocks (Wheatstone 1867 / Wadsworth 1817 / Urkryptografen) | `M(S(M′(p)))`, state = cumulative hand steps (plaintext-history dependent) | **Ciphertext doubles ⟺ plaintext-ring-adjacent pairs** (Friedman's leak); doubles disguised by procedure (Wheatstone: second letter → Q); mixed plaintext rings exist (Urkryptografen preprinted rings) | Exact clock mechanics (plaintext-sum state) **excluded** by anchor constraint tests (repo `model_power`: Alberti/psum contradictions); the *factored form* is our model |
| Gromark / running numeric key | `c = C[(p + k_t)]`, `k` from lagged-Fibonacci primer | Aperiodic like progressive; key stream has small-digit structure; NCID separates it from `progressive_key` statistically | **Open at the margin** — subsumed under "general per-triplet K_g[t]"; linear drift currently sufficient (keyspace ledger) |
| Autokey (PT or CT) | key extends with plaintext / ciphertext | CT-autokey: error/state propagation, **re-sync events impossible** | **Excluded** — 5 clean re-sync events E1/W1 (`resync`); PT-autokey excluded by anchor constraint counts |
| Chaocipher-class dynamic alphabets | alphabets permuted after each letter as f(history) | Isomorphs only between **messages in depth**, never at within-message offsets; broken via divide-and-conquer + IoC on in-depth traffic (Lasry et al. 2016) | **Excluded** — our isomorphs occur at within-message offsets with constant q-difference, impossible if the alphabet mutates with history |
| Fractionation (bifid/trifid/fractionated Morse) | sub-symbol mixing | per-digit stream structure; period seams | **Excluded** — per-eye-mark streams uniform, digit-transpose sweep clean (repo) |
| Homophonic | one letter → several symbols | flattened unigrams but **language-rate repeats/bigram texture** (the Z340 route) | **Excluded** — repeat texture is isomorphic, not homophonic (z≈117 isomorph evidence requires interrelated alphabets) |

The taxonomy places us precisely: **aperiodic-key, fixed-interrelated-alphabet
substitution — the progressive/clock branch — with both the plaintext embedding
and the ciphertext alphabet mixed** (the literature's acknowledged hard
configuration).

## 3. The identification battery the professionals use

From the ACA/NCID/practical tradition, cipher-type identification from ciphertext
alone rests on a stack of statistics, in rough order of power:

1. **Unigram IoC / entropy** — separates mono (language-rough) from poly (flat).
   Ours: flat at the uniform-83 floor, κ ≈ 0.012 (repo).
2. **Kasiski + periodic IoC** — finds a repeating-key period. Quagmire I–IV are
   *defined* by having one ("all four quagmire variants are vulnerable to Kasiski
   examination… because they all use repeating indicator keywords"). Ours: none →
   off the periodic branch entirely.
3. **Kappa (coincidence) between messages in depth** — Kullback's depth test; the
   Chaocipher break ran on exactly this (in-depth messages, IoC-driven
   divide-and-conquer). Ours: within-triplet aligned kappa z = 20.4 (E1/W1) and
   9.4 (E4/E5); cross-triplet ≈ 0 (repo `keystream_scope`) → per-triplet keys.
4. **Isomorph census** — the machine-era tell (Purple; Chaocipher in depth) for
   interrelated alphabets. Ours: z ≈ 117 (repo), including within-message offset
   isomorphs, which additionally kill history-dependent alphabets (§2).
5. **Coincidence spectrum by distance** (`c[t] = c[t+d]` counts) — under a
   sliding cipher each distance d probes one *diagonal* of the joint
   (text-distance × code-difference) plaintext distribution; under a periodic key
   it probes key periodicity. This is the micro-tool that does the new narrowing
   below.
6. **Doubles as a family tell** — for clock ciphers, Friedman (1918, via Kaeding)
   identified that ciphertext doubles leak plaintext-ring adjacency, and
   operating procedure *disguised* plaintext doubles (Wheatstone: second letter
   replaced by Q). Zero-doubles is thus a documented clock/progressive-family
   signature, not an anomaly.
7. **ML type-ID as a calibration standard** — NCID's ensembles identify 55 ACA
   types at ~80–83% accuracy from 100-character samples using engineered
   statistical features + learned features. Relevance to us is methodological:
   these fingerprints demonstrably suffice to *type* a cipher at lengths far
   below ours (1036 glyphs), which is why the family identification here is
   considered settled even though the key is not.

## 4. New measurements and what they narrow (this cycle)

### 4a. The coincidence spectrum, measured

Pooled over all 9 messages (script in §5):

```
 d   obs   exp(uniform)     z
 1     0      12.37      -3.54     <- zero doubles
 2     5      12.27      -2.09
 3     9      12.16      -0.91
 4    26      12.05      +4.04     <- the hot diagonal (2.16x)
 5    11      11.94      -0.27
 6    12      11.83      +0.05
 7    12      11.72      +0.08
 8    11      11.61      -0.18     <- flat where a period-4 key would spike
 9    18      11.51      +1.93     (sub-threshold; watch)
10-12  ~flat
```

Under the linear class, `c[t]=c[t+d]` ⟺ the plaintext codes at distance d differ
by exactly `−σ·drift·d`. Each row is therefore one diagonal slice of the
plaintext's (distance × code-difference) joint distribution — the spectrum is a
**plaintext/embedding probe**, not noise.

### 4b. Zero doubles excludes the consecutive-block embedding

If the plaintext letters occupied a consecutive block of ℤ₈₃ (the "straight
ring"), then for every (σ, drift = ±1) combination, ciphertext doubles occur at
the language's **alphabet-adjacent-pair** rate. Measured on our corpora:

```
English  : descending-adjacent 4.53%  -> predicts ~47 doubles in 1027 pairs
           ascending-adjacent  4.69%  -> predicts ~48
Finnish  : descending-adjacent 3.08%  -> predicts ~32
           ascending-adjacent  3.96%  -> predicts ~41
OBSERVED : 0        (Poisson P(0 | ≥32) < 10⁻¹³)
```

**Verdict: consecutive-block embedding × {English, Finnish} × all four
σ·drift = ±1 members is excluded.** This retro-explains part of the
`alphabet_sweep` null (its frames assumed structured/consecutive plaintext
labelings) and leaves two live readings, both historically instantiated:

- **Scattered embedding (mixed plaintext ring).** If ~26–30 letter codes are
  scattered among 83 slots, adjacent-code pairs rarely land on two co-occurring
  letters, and doubles ≈ 0 automatically. Urkryptografen shipped with *preprinted
  mixed plaintext rings* — the configuration Friedman recommended and Kaeding
  couldn't attack. This is the parsimonious reading and it sharpens the target:
  we are hunting a **double-mixed clock**, i.e. both `E` and `C` unstructured.
- **Doubles-disguise procedure.** Wheatstone's own instructions disguise
  plaintext doubles (second letter → Q) because the device cannot encode them;
  Wadsworth-class usage advances a full revolution so "double letters never
  appear in the ciphertext." An author imitating clock procedure would produce
  a zero-doubles ciphertext by *convention*. Note this convention suppresses a
  different pair set than the linear-drift leak does, so on its own it explains
  d=1 ≈ 0 only under near-drift-0 mechanics; treat it as the minority reading.

### 4c. The d=4 / d=8 discriminator

The 2.16× excess at d=4 with a **flat d=8** cuts two ways:

- Against a periodic keystream component: a period-4 element in a general
  `K_g[t]` would elevate *every* multiple of 4 (classic periodic-IoC logic);
  d=8 sits at expectation. So the spectrum shape mildly **disfavors short-period
  structure in K** and is consistent with pure linear drift.
- For the plaintext/embedding reading: under linear drift the excess means one
  or a few **frequent letter pairs at text-distance 4 whose code difference is
  exactly ∓4** — a single hot diagonal cell, i.e. one soft equation on `E`
  involving high-frequency letters. That is a crumb of embedding structure,
  recoverable the moment any anchor orients the code axis. (d=9 at z=1.9 is
  logged as a second candidate cell, below threshold.)

### 4d. Gauge equivalence — what "narrowing σ and drift" can even mean

σ = −1 is absorbed by relabeling `E → −E`; drift sign is absorbed by reflecting
`C` and `E` jointly; bases are key rotations (Kaeding's cut-point observation).
So the class members {pmp, pure, beaufort} are **gauge images of one another**,
which is the same fact `model_power` proved empirically (indistinguishable on
isomorph evidence). The narrowable objects are gauge-invariants only: the
spectrum diagonals (products σ·drift·d), the embedding's scatter (4b), and the
per-triplet base/key structure. Ciphertext-only campaigns to "prove it's Beaufort"
are category errors; the docs should stop treating σ/drift as open empirical
questions and start treating them as coordinate choices.

## 5. Reproduce the measurements

```python
import json, math
d = json.load(open('noita_eye_core/corpus.json')); N = d['deck_size']
cts = [list(c) for c in d['ciphertexts']]
for dd in range(1, 13):
    obs = sum(1 for ct in cts for t in range(len(ct)-dd) if ct[t] == ct[t+dd])
    npairs = sum(max(0, len(ct)-dd) for ct in cts)
    exp = npairs / N; z = (obs-exp) / math.sqrt(exp*(1-1/N))
    print(dd, obs, round(exp,2), round(z,2))
```

Language adjacent-pair rates: count `pos[next] == pos[prev] ∓ 1` over
`corpora/english_big.txt` and `corpora/kalevala_finnish_clean.txt` (script in
session log; rates above). Plain double-letter rates measured en 2.83%, fi 6.97%
(relevant to any drift-0/general-K discussion: Finnish plaintext under a
doubles-transparent cipher would show ~70 doubles; we see 0).

## 6. Methodology imports (for the search side)

1. **Rotation-invariant scoring (from Kaeding).** His "outer fitness" scores
   candidate keys against n-gram tables compiled from a *stream-enciphered*
   corpus, with every polygram canonicalized by shifting its first symbol to a
   fixed origin — the statistic is invariant to the rotation gauge, so the
   search never wastes capacity fitting bases, and "constraints on the key are
   more evenly spread over it." Our `order_anneal` objective should be checked
   against this standard: score decrypts in **difference-canonicalized** n-gram
   space (subtract the first code of each n-gram) so per-message `base_m` drops
   out of the objective by construction. This is a concrete candidate for why
   the anneal floor ≈ shuffled floor: an objective with 9 free bases in it has
   exactly the surplus freedom the overfit control detects.
2. **Depth-first, divide-and-conquer (from the Chaocipher break).** Lasry et
   al. cracked in-depth Chaocipher traffic ciphertext-only with a
   divide-and-conquer hill climb driven by IoC, and a known-plaintext variant
   for *short* in-depth segments. Our per-triplet depth (136 exploitable 2-deep
   positions) is the analogous asset; any new search should climb on the depth
   residuals, not on whole messages.
3. **Metaheuristic hygiene (Lasry 2018).** The dissertation-length treatment of
   hill climbing / simulated annealing for classical ciphers — scoring functions
   matched to the key-recovery stage, restart discipline, smooth fitness
   landscapes — is the standing reference for making our anneal trustworthy;
   it demonstrably broke systems (Playfair, double transposition, ADFGVX,
   M-209, Enigma double indicators) that resisted prior automation.
4. **Type-ID battery as a dashboard (NCID).** Generalize the engineered feature
   set to N = 83 and pin it as a standing `cipher_data` dashboard so any future
   model proposal is auto-checked against the full battery, not just the
   statistic it was invented to fit.

## 7. Standing conclusions for the formula

SOLID: the family is the aperiodic progressive/clock branch with interrelated
fixed alphabets; periodic, autokey, dynamic-alphabet, fractionated, and
homophonic branches are excluded by the battery in §3. NEW-SOLID (this cycle):
the plaintext embedding is **not a consecutive block** — the system is
double-mixed, the literature's hard case (§4b). SUPPORTED: keystream is linear
drift (no short-period K component; §4c). REFRAMED: σ/drift are gauge
coordinates, not discoverable facts (§4d). The bottleneck remains the external
anchor or correct crib, exactly as the clock literature predicts for
double-mixed devices — with our depth and isomorph assets as the levers the
historical attackers lacked.

